# whittle

Lint and auto-normalize [Conventional Commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) subjects. Plugs into [`pre-commit`](https://pre-commit.com/), [`prek`](https://github.com/j178/prek), [`husky`](https://typicode.github.io/husky/), and [`lefthook`](https://github.com/evilmartians/lefthook) as a `commit-msg` hook.

`whittle` parses a commit message, applies a configurable set of transforms (lowercase, strip noise chars, replace `and` with `&`, normalize scope separators, …), drops body + trailers, and finally validates the result against your rules. If the message can't be brought into compliance, the hook fails.

## Install

### pre-commit / prek

Add to your `.pre-commit-config.yaml`:

```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/smarlhens/whittle
    rev: v0.1.0
    hooks:
      - id: whittle-fix
```

Then install the `commit-msg` stage hook:

```sh
prek install --hook-type commit-msg
# or
pre-commit install --hook-type commit-msg
```

### husky

Install the [npm wrapper](./npm) (downloads the matching binary on install):

```sh
npm install --save-dev @smarlhens/whittle
npx husky add .husky/commit-msg 'npx whittle fix "$1"'
```

### lefthook (`lefthook.yml`)

```sh
npm install --save-dev @smarlhens/whittle
```

```yaml
commit-msg:
  commands:
    whittle:
      run: npx whittle fix {1}
```

### Cargo / Homebrew / direct binary

```sh
# Rust toolchain
cargo install whittle

# Or download a prebuilt binary
# https://github.com/smarlhens/whittle/releases
```

Once `whittle` is on `$PATH`, wire it up manually:

```sh
# .husky/commit-msg or .git/hooks/commit-msg
#!/bin/sh
whittle fix "$1"
```

## Defaults

Out of the box, `whittle-fix` applies these transforms to the commit subject:

| Component   | Transform                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| type        | lowercase                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
| scope       | lowercase, `/` → `-`, `\` → `-`                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
| description | lowercase, `and` → `&`, normalize smart quotes (`“ ” ‘ ’` → `" '`), em/en dashes (`— –`) → `-`, collapse runs of `!` or `?`, strip `/ \ [ ] { }`, strip standalone dots (keep version dots like `1.2.3`), strip trailing dot, collapse whitespace |
| body        | dropped                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| footers     | dropped (denies `Co-Authored-By` and `Co-authored-by` by default)                                                                                                                                                                                 |

Every one of those transforms is reported, with its before and after value — see
[Diagnostic codes](#diagnostic-codes). Use `whittle check` to see them before they
are applied, `whittle fix` to apply them and be told what happened.

Validation rules:

- Must parse as Conventional Commits.
- Type must be one of: `feat, fix, refactor, perf, docs, test, chore, build, ci, style, revert`.
- Subject ≤ 72 characters.

## Configuration

Point `whittle` at a `whittle.toml`:

```yaml
hooks:
  - id: whittle-fix
    args: [--config, whittle.toml]
```

Example `whittle.toml` that mirrors the defaults:

```toml
[type]
lowercase = true

[scope]
lowercase = true
replace = [
  { from = "/",  to = "-" },
  { from = "\\", to = "-" },
]

[description]
lowercase = true
collapse_whitespace = true
trailing_dot = "strip"             # keep | strip
strip_chars = ["/", "\\", "[", "]", "{", "}"]
internal_dots = "keep_in_numbers"  # all | none | keep_in_numbers
replace = [
  { from = '\band\b', to = "&", regex = true },
  { from = "“",  to = "\"" },             # “ → "
  { from = "”",  to = "\"" },             # ” → "
  { from = "‘",  to = "'" },              # ‘ → '
  { from = "’",  to = "'" },              # ’ → '
  { from = "—",  to = "-" },              # — → -
  { from = "–",  to = "-" },              # – → -
  { from = "!{2,}",   to = "!", regex = true },
  { from = '\?{2,}',  to = "?", regex = true },
]

[body]
keep = false

[footers]
keep = false
deny = ["Co-Authored-By", "Co-authored-by"]

[rules]
max_subject_length = 72
require_conventional = true
allowed_types = [
  "feat", "fix", "refactor", "perf", "docs", "test",
  "chore", "build", "ci", "style", "revert",
]
```

Every `regex = true` pattern in a `replace` table is compiled when the config
loads, so a typo fails the hook immediately instead of silently doing nothing
while `whittle rules` keeps advertising it as enforced.

## CLI

```sh
whittle check <file>   # report what fix would change, never write, exit 1 if anything is pending
whittle fix   <file>   # apply transforms in place, report what changed, then validate
whittle rules          # print the active ruleset as instructions (for CLAUDE.md / an agent)
whittle --config whittle.toml fix .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
whittle --format json check .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
```

Both modes run the same normalization. They differ in who owns the wording:

|                               | `check`                                                  | `fix`                                              |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Writes the file               | never                                                    | yes                                                |
| Reports each rewrite          | yes, as pending (`description:`, `would be removed`)     | yes, as done (`description rewritten:`, `removed`) |
| Prints the normalized message | yes, unless it would be illegible or a violation remains | no — it is already on disk                         |
| Exit 1 on a pending rewrite   | yes                                                      | no                                                 |
| Exit 1 on a rule violation    | yes                                                      | yes                                                |

`fix` is the convenient hook: it silently made your message conform, and now tells
you what it did. `check` is the mode for an agent or a reviewer — it refuses to
guess at your intent, and hands back the message it would have written so the
caller can adopt or rework it.

Both judge the message by its **semantic content**: the message with git's comment
block and scissors section stripped and surrounding whitespace trimmed. So
`check` passes a `git commit -v` message whose subject is already clean, even
though the file also holds the comment block and the diff. `fix` additionally
canonicalizes that formatting when it writes; `check` ignores it, because git
strips comments itself and flagging them would fail every real commit.

### Using `check` with an agent

`whittle fix` rewrites subjects and drops bodies and trailers by design. That is
fine for a human who accepts the house style, but an agent writing a commit
message has no way to learn the rules from a silent rewrite — and a dropped
`Co-Authored-By` or body is lost work. `check` closes that loop:

```console
$ whittle check .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
{{ whittle(cmd="check", input="ci: restore actions/checkout@v6 in dist-generated release.yml\n\nWhy this matters.\nMore lines.\n\nCo-Authored-By: A <a@b>\n") }}
```

No `suggested message` here, because normalizing this subject would weld
`actions/checkout` and `release.yml` into nonsense — see
[`check` will not hand back an illegible subject](#check-will-not-hand-back-an-illegible-subject).
A subject that only needs cosmetic tidying does get one.

Two halves matter here. The diagnostics say what _changed_; the `how to write`
block says what to _write instead_. A caller shown only rewrites learns nothing and
makes the same mistake next commit, so only the rules the message actually tripped
are listed, phrased as instructions. `fix` prints the same block — it exits 0, but
the reasons still reach the caller.

#### `check` will not hand back an illegible subject

Some normalizations delete characters from inside a word, which destroys meaning
rather than tidying it:

| You wrote                                    | `fix` writes                               |
| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `handle src/lib.rs parse error`              | `handle srclibrs parse error`              |
| `bump @actions/foo-bar to 1.2.3`             | `bump @actionsfoo-bar to 1.2.3`            |
| `restore actions/checkout@v6 in release.yml` | `restore actionscheckout@v6 in releaseyml` |

For a human running `fix`, that is the house style and it is applied as configured.
But there is no sense in which `srclibrs` is a _correct_ subject, so `check`
refuses to offer it. It names each ruined token and asks for a rewording instead:

```console
$ whittle check .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
{{ whittle(cmd="check", input="fix: handle src/lib.rs parse error\n") }}
```

No `suggested message` is printed, so an agent cannot shortcut past the problem — it
has to say the same thing in words, e.g. `fix: handle parse error in the lib module`.
Edge trimming is not mangling: `Bump A and B.` still gets the ordinary
`suggested message: chore: bump a & b`, because `b.` -> `b` loses nothing.

This is a `check`-only behaviour. `fix` keeps applying your rules exactly as
configured, so nothing changes for the human path.

Exit code 1 aborts the commit. Wire it up as `whittle-check` instead of
`whittle-fix`:

```yaml
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/smarlhens/whittle
    rev: v0.2.0 # `check` reports pending rewrites from v0.2.0 onward
    hooks:
      - id: whittle-check
```

> Pin `v0.2.0` or later. In `v0.1.x` `check` only validated the subject and
> never reported pending rewrites, so on an older tag mangled subjects and
> dropped trailers pass with exit 0.

### Using `check` with Claude Code

No Claude-specific configuration is needed: install `whittle-check` as a
`commit-msg` hook and the loop closes by itself. Claude runs `git commit` through
its Bash tool, git runs the hook, the non-zero exit aborts the commit, and the
hook's stderr comes back in the tool result — so Claude reads the rules it broke
and retries with a compliant subject.

A `commit-msg` hook is the right seam rather than a Claude Code `PreToolUse` hook,
because it receives the actual message file. A `PreToolUse` hook only sees the
shell command string, which would mean parsing `-m`, repeated `-m`, `--message=`,
`-F`, and the `-m "$(cat <<'EOF' … EOF)"` heredoc form Claude Code normally uses —
and it would still miss editor-based commits entirely.

To skip the failed first attempt altogether, give the rules to the agent up front.
`whittle rules` prints them in a form meant for pasting into `CLAUDE.md`:

```console
$ whittle rules
{{ whittle(cmd="rules") }}
```

Every line is derived from your `whittle.toml` — including the `replace` tables,
which is why a project that inverts a default rule is never told the opposite of
what it enforces. If you pass `--config`, pass it here too
(`whittle --config whittle.toml rules`); there is no config discovery, so a bare
invocation prints the built-in defaults. Failure output on stderr always names the
right command for the run.

Either paste it into `CLAUDE.md`, or inject it every session with a `SessionStart`
hook, whose stdout Claude reads as context:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": "whittle rules" }]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

`whittle --format json rules` returns the same list as
`{"version":1,"ok":true,"rules":[…]}` if you would rather build the prompt
yourself. A bad config still yields JSON (`ok:false` plus a `whittle.failed`
diagnostic) rather than empty stdout.

### JSON output

`--format json` writes a report to **stdout** (diagnostics stay on stderr in
`human` mode), for tooling that wants the data rather than the prose:

```console
$ whittle --format json check .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG
{{ whittle(cmd="check", format="json", input="Chore: Bump A and B.\n\nbody text\n") }}
```

Contract:

- `version` is bumped only on a breaking change to the payload.
- `ok` is `true` exactly when the process exits `0` — never read one without the
  other agreeing. In particular a `fix` that rewrote the subject and succeeded is
  `ok: true` with a non-empty `diagnostics`.
- `original` is the message with git's comment block and scissors section removed
  and surrounding whitespace trimmed.
- `normalized` is the message to adopt, or **`null`** when there is nothing
  adoptable: an unparseable message, one whose normalization would not itself
  parse, or one whose normalization would be illegible (a
  `subject.needs-rewording` violation). Never echo a `null` back as a commit
  message — reword instead.
- `kind` says what the diagnostic asks of you. `rewrite`: `before` becomes
  `after`. `removal`: `before` is deleted, `after` is null. `violation`: `before`
  is rejected and nothing was written — an `after` here is illustrative (what
  normalization _would_ have produced) and must never be adopted.
- `applied` is `true` once the change is on disk (`fix`), `false` while pending
  (`check`).
- `guidance` is the rules this message tripped, phrased as instructions — the
  machine-readable form of the `how to write` block.

### Diagnostic codes

`warning` means whittle can rewrite it; `error` means it cannot, and the message
must change.

| Code                               | Severity | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| ---------------------------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `type.lowercased`                  | warning  | type was not lowercase (`[type].lowercase`)                                                                                                                                                                       |
| `scope.lowercased`                 | warning  | scope was not lowercase                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| `scope.replaced`                   | warning  | a `[scope].replace` rule matched                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| `description.lowercased`           | warning  | description was not lowercase                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| `description.replaced`             | warning  | a `[description].replace` rule matched                                                                                                                                                                            |
| `description.chars-stripped`       | warning  | a character in `strip_chars` was removed                                                                                                                                                                          |
| `description.dots-stripped`        | warning  | a dot was removed per `internal_dots`                                                                                                                                                                             |
| `description.trailing-dot`         | warning  | trailing dot removed — only reachable with `internal_dots = "all"`; otherwise `description.dots-stripped` already removes it first                                                                                |
| `description.whitespace-collapsed` | warning  | a run of whitespace was collapsed to one space                                                                                                                                                                    |
| `description.trimmed`              | warning  | leading or trailing whitespace removed                                                                                                                                                                            |
| `body.dropped`                     | warning  | body removed (`[body].keep = false`)                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `footers.dropped`                  | warning  | all footers removed (`[footers].keep = false`)                                                                                                                                                                    |
| `footer.denied`                    | warning  | one footer matched `[footers].deny`, other footers are kept                                                                                                                                                       |
| `message.reformatted`              | warning  | the message differs only structurally (blank lines, footer spacing, a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer becoming `!`) — no `transform_*` rule fired                                                                       |
| `type.disallowed`                  | error    | type is not in `allowed_types`                                                                                                                                                                                    |
| `subject.too-long`                 | error    | subject exceeds `max_subject_length`                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `commit.not-conventional`          | error    | message does not parse as a Conventional Commit                                                                                                                                                                   |
| `normalize.invalid-result`         | error    | normalizing would produce an unparseable message; file left untouched                                                                                                                                             |
| `whittle.failed`                   | error    | whittle could not run (unreadable file, malformed config, or an invalid `regex = true` pattern)                                                                                                                   |
| `subject.needs-rewording`          | error    | `strip_chars`/`internal_dots` would weld two words together (`release.yml` → `releaseyml`); `check` only, one per ruined token — never raised for a `.replace` rule, which is the project's own deliberate choice |

